==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 09 NOTES — ncdu
Date: 23-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- ncdu is du with an interactive screen: scan, sort by size, step into folders until you find the hog
- Safe habit on system folders: ncdu -r -x (read-only so d can't delete, one filesystem only)
- Keys: s sort size, n sort name, g graph, i info, d delete (only in my own lab folder), q quit
- A scan can be saved with -o and opened later with -f, so root can scan and I can view without root
- df -h shows space per filesystem, df -i shows inodes - a disk can be full of tiny files with space free
- du/ncdu only see files that still have a path; df counts everything actually used
- A deleted-but-still-open file (Day 02 lsof) keeps using space: df stays high, ncdu can't see it,
  sudo lsof +L1 finds it, and the space returns when the process lets go
- Logs in /var/log are the classic hog, and logrotate (Day 07) is what keeps them bounded

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] install ncdu, df -h, df -i
[ ] du --max-depth=1 | sort -h on /var
[ ] sudo ncdu -r -x /var/log and reading the biggest items
[ ] building ~/ncdu-lab and exploring it with ncdu
[ ] deleting a big file from inside ncdu (lab folder only)
[ ] (bonus) saving a scan with -o and opening it with -f
[ ] the deleted-but-open trap: rm, df, lsof +L1, kill, df again

paste or describe what you saw :
$ sudo du -h --max-depth=1 /var 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -8
>

$ sudo ncdu -r -x /var/log  (the biggest items on screen)
>

$ df -h ~ | tail -1   (before rm / after rm / after kill)
>

$ sudo lsof +L1 | grep big.bin
>


